CAT = "o30Electrical"; SEG = "ELE"
Q = [
("Under the crane power line rule's Table A, what is the minimum clearance from a line over 50 kV up to 200 kV?",
 "15 feet", ["10 feet", "20 feet", "25 feet"],
 "Table A in 29 CFR 1926.1408 sets 10 feet up to 50 kV, 15 feet over 50 to 200 kV, 20 feet over 200 to 350 kV and more for higher voltages."),
("A crane must have power line precautions planned if any part, swung to its maximum working radius, could get closer to a line than:",
 "20 feet", ["10 feet", "50 feet", "6 feet"],
 "1926.1408(a)(2) asks whether the equipment, load line or load could get closer than 20 feet to a power line; if so the employer must deenergize and ground, keep 20 feet, or use the Table A distance with precautions."),
("When crane encroachment precautions are required, who must attend the planning meeting?",
 "Operator and nearby workers", ["Only the utility's lineman", "Only the property owner", "The OSHA area director"],
 "1926.1408(b)(1) requires a planning meeting with the operator and the other workers who will be in the area to review the line's location and the steps to prevent encroachment."),
("Near a power line, if the crane operator cannot see the elevated warning line, the employer must also use:",
 "A dedicated spotter", ["A second crane operator", "A road flagger", "Rubber outrigger pads"],
 "1926.1408(b)(3) requires a dedicated spotter, in addition to another listed measure, when the operator cannot see the elevated warning line."),
("Before work begins, how must the employer find out whether energized circuits are where the work could reach them?",
 "Ask, observe or use instruments", ["Read the original building plans only", "Wait for the first test cut", "Rely on the foreman's memory"],
 "1926.416(a)(3) requires the employer to ascertain by inquiry, direct observation or instruments whether any exposed or concealed energized circuit is located where the work could contact it."),
("Where a buried line's location is unknown, workers using jackhammers or bars that could hit it must get:",
 "Insulated protective gloves", ["Steel-toe safety boots", "Hearing protection", "Double-layer leather work gloves"],
 "1926.416(a)(2) requires insulated protective gloves for employees using jackhammers, bars or similar hand tools that may contact a line of unknown location."),
("Temporary job site lights may hang from their own electric cords only when:",
 "Both are built for it", ["The cord is a heavy-duty type", "The light is under 100 watts", "A GFCI protects the circuit"],
 "1926.405(a)(2)(ii)(F) says temporary lights shall not be suspended by their electric cords unless the cords and lights are designed for that means of suspension."),
("Temporary wiring on a construction project must be removed:",
 "When its purpose is done", ["Within 90 days of installing it", "At the end of every shift", "Only before the final inspection"],
 "1926.405(a)(2)(i) requires temporary wiring to be removed immediately upon completion of construction or of the purpose for which it was installed."),
("Which flexible cord may be repaired with a splice that keeps its insulation and outer sheath properties?",
 "Hard-service cord, No. 12 or larger", ["Any cord shorter than 50 feet long", "Junior hard-service cord", "Any cord with a ground pin"],
 "1926.405(g)(2)(iii) requires continuous cord lengths without splices, except that hard-service cords No. 12 or larger may be spliced if the splice keeps the cord's insulation, sheath and usage characteristics."),
("Strain relief where a cord enters a plug or device prevents a pull from reaching:",
 "Joints and terminal screws", ["The GFCI test button", "The outlet's faceplate", "The breaker in the main panel"],
 "1926.405(g)(2)(iv) requires cords to be connected so strain relief keeps pull from being transmitted directly to joints or terminal screws."),
("Each disconnecting means for a motor or appliance must be:",
 "Legibly marked for its purpose", ["Painted bright red", "Locked open whenever unattended", "Mounted within 3 feet of the floor"],
 "1926.403(h) requires each disconnecting means to be legibly marked to indicate its purpose, unless its purpose is evident from where it is."),
("Which current path through the body is generally the most dangerous?",
 "Across the chest and heart", ["Through one fingertip", "Along one forearm", "Down one leg to the ground"],
 "A path from hand to hand or hand to foot crosses the chest, where current can disturb the heart's rhythm. Paths that stay in one limb rarely reach the heart."),
("A coworker is locked onto an energized cord and cannot let go. What should you do first?",
 "Shut off power at the source", ["Grab their belt and pull", "Splash water on the cord", "Wait for the breaker to trip"],
 "Touching the victim puts you in the circuit. De-energize first, or if that is impossible, push the person clear with a dry nonconductive object, then call for help."),
("Before using a portable GFCI on a job site, a worker should:",
 "Press its test button", ["Tape over the reset button", "Remove its ground prong", "Plug it into another GFCI"],
 "A GFCI can fail without warning, so manufacturers and OSHA guidance call for testing it with the test button before use. Removing a ground prong defeats protection."),
("If a vehicle touching a power line catches fire and the operator must get out, the safest way is to:",
 "Jump clear and shuffle away", ["Climb down holding the rail", "Run away in long strides", "Step off with one foot first"],
 "Jumping clear keeps the body from bridging the vehicle and the ground. Shuffling with feet together limits step voltage in the energized ground around the vehicle."),
("Near exposed energized parts, which tool is unsafe to use?",
 "Metal measuring tape", ["Fiberglass ladder", "Insulated hand tools", "Nonconductive rope"],
 "OSHA's electrical safe work practices bar conductive articles such as metal tapes and rules where they could contact exposed energized parts. Fiberglass and insulated tools do not conduct."),
("Under 1926.417, controls that are deactivated while work is done on equipment or circuits must be:",
 "Tagged", ["Painted yellow", "Removed from the panel", "Taped in the on position"],
 "1926.417(a) requires controls deactivated during work on energized or deenergized equipment or circuits to be tagged, and (b) requires deenergized equipment to be made inoperative and tagged wherever it can be energized."),
("Many serious injuries from shocks on ladders and roofs are actually caused by:",
 "Falls after the shock", ["Burns from the arc flash", "Hearing damage from the blast", "Eye injuries"],
 "OSHA's electrical training notes that even a small shock can make a worker lose balance, so a fall to a lower level is often the injury that kills or disables."),
("When carrying a long ladder or pipe near overhead lines, the safest way to hold it is:",
 "Lowered and horizontal", ["Upright, for better balance and grip", "Tilted toward the lines", "Raised above the head"],
 "Keeping long conductive objects low and level keeps them far from overhead lines. Carrying them upright is a common way workers contact lines."),
("Electrical equipment and conductors used on a construction site must be:",
 "Approved, as by a test lab", ["Bought new for each project", "Painted to show its voltage", "Checked by OSHA each year"],
 "1926.403(a) requires all electrical conductors and equipment to be approved, which in practice means listed or labeled by a nationally recognized testing laboratory."),
]
